    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d313">

                <pb facs="0428" n="322" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.6131/377</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Sayre</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-14">January 14, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Ambassador Bullitt, in the course of a
                    conversation concerning the situation in Russia, spoke, among other subjects, of
                    the trade situation. He adverted to the agreement entered into last June [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">July</hi>] under which the United States agreed to
                    extend to Russia most-favored-nation treatment in return for an agreement by
                    Russia to increase its purchase of American goods so as not to fall below a
                    certain amount. Mr. Bullitt said that he
                    felt confident that Russia is in a position to buy large quantities of American
                    goods. Russia lacks and needs American goods. Mr. Bullitt gave it as his opinion that if the present agreement
                    should be renewed for another year Russia should be asked <pb facs="0429"
                        n="323" xml:id="pg323"/> to increase substantially the amount of American
                    goods to be bought by her. He suggested that in his opinion we should ask Russia
                    to buy a minimum of $50,000,000 worth of American goods for the year.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">rancis</hi>] B.
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sayre</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
